    "mtn.haba.intro" to "Haba sits in Shangri-La, Yunnan, across Tiger Leaping Gorge from Yulong. It is often called a first snow mountain: above the snowline the slope is gentle, there are no crevasses, and the technical difficulty is low. But from Haba village at 2,600 m the route gains nearly 2,800 m in a single day, and altitude sickness is the real obstacle. In Naxi, haba means flower of gold.",
    "mtn.everest.intro" to "The highest point on Earth, and the definition of the word altitude itself. The two standard routes have entirely different characters: the south side threads through the Khumbu Icefall, while the north begins at a base camp at the end of a road at 5,200 m. Hillary and Tenzing made the first ascent from the south in 1953, and who stepped first is still unsettled — Tenzing only ever said we did. What stops people is not any technical pitch but how long you can stay above 8,000 m.",
    "mtn.k2.intro" to "Second highest, and by consensus the hardest. The pyramid has no genuinely easy line: House's Chimney and the Black Pyramid on the Abruzzi Ridge are fixed obstacles. An Italian team made the first ascent in 1954, and for half a century afterwards its death rate stayed above Everest's. Climbers call it the Savage Mountain, and that is not a figure of speech.",
    "mtn.kangchenjunga.intro" to "Third highest, standing on the border between Nepal and Sikkim. When the British team made the first ascent in 1955, they stopped a few metres below the summit — Sikkimese belief holds the top to be the seat of a god, not to be trodden. The tradition continues: most summiters still stop one step short.",
    "mtn.lhotse.intro" to "Separated from Everest by the South Col and sharing the same base camp and much of the route, so it is often treated as a bonus peak on the way. But its own west face is a near-3,000 m continuous wall, first climbed frontally by a Russian team only in 1990. It is the clearest example of one mountain with two very different lives.",
    "mtn.makalu.intro" to "A near-perfect four-sided pyramid standing alone 20 km southeast of Everest. Its southeast ridge is one of the longest continuous ridges among the 8,000ers, running from 5,000 m to the top; the French first ascent in 1955 placed seven camps along it. It rarely appears on first-8,000 lists because the route is simply that long.",
    "mtn.chooyu.intro" to "The beginner's 8,000er — not because it is short, but because it is gentle. The northwest ridge is a snow slope around thirty degrees, and it has the highest proportion of commercial clients of the fourteen. But easiest is always relative: it trades technical difficulty for endurance and time, and nothing above 8,000 m is truly easy.",
    "mtn.dhaulagiri.intro" to "The name comes from Sanskrit, white mountain. Both the south and west faces drop more than 4,000 m; the 1960 Swiss-Austrian first ascent took the northeast ridge, and nobody touched the south face for twenty years. It faces Annapurna across the Kali Gandaki, thirty kilometres apart — the deepest gorge on Earth.",
    "mtn.manaslu.intro" to "From the Sanskrit for mountain of the spirit, and only confirmed as an 8,000er in the 1950s. A Japanese team opened the normal route on the northeast face in 1956, and for decades it was the backup choice for a last 8,000er. Commercial traffic surged in the 2010s and the death rate rose with it — too many people treated it as the easy one.",
    "mtn.nangaparbat.intro" to "Nicknamed the naked mountain. The Rupal Face is the highest continuous rock wall on Earth, close to 4,600 m from valley to summit. Hermann Buhl soloed the first ascent in 1953 and bivouacked at 8,000 m on the descent, without tent or oxygen. That effort is still regarded as one of mountaineering's great solo gambles, and he never returned to the mountain.",
    "mtn.annapurna.intro" to "The first 8,000er ever climbed, by a French team in 1950, three years before Everest. But its death rate has long been the highest of the fourteen: avalanches and seracs on the south face are the main killers. The first ascent team set out without even knowing the mountain's exact height, and the figure they brought back was three hundred metres above what the maps said.",
    "mtn.gasherbrum1.intro" to "Also called Hidden Peak — from most angles the summit cannot be seen. An international team first tried it in 1934; an American team made the ascent in 1958. It sits deep in the Karakoram, ten days of glacier walking from the road, so most of the energy is spent before base camp. That is why it ranks eleventh yet sees far fewer ascents than its neighbours.",
    "mtn.broad.intro" to "Named for its 1.5 km wide summit — which is exactly the trap. Many people think they have topped out when two more hours along the snow ridge remain. An Austrian team made the first ascent in 1957. It shares a base camp with Gasherbrum II, making it one of the few Karakoram combinations where two 8,000ers come in one trip.",
    "mtn.gasherbrum2.intro" to "One of the least technical 8,000ers, first climbed by an Austrian team in 1956. The southwest ridge is gentle and holds almost no hard pitches. But the approach crosses the Baltoro, one of the longest non-polar glaciers on Earth; simply walking in takes a week. That is why it is often left as the final one to tick off.",
    "mtn.shishapangma.intro" to "The only 8,000er entirely inside China. A Chinese team made the first ascent in 1964, and it was the last of the fourteen to be climbed — not because it is hard, but because access was closed for years. Its north side runs up grass slopes to 6,000 m; the Nepalese south side is a steep rock wall. One mountain, two countries, two characters.",
    "mtn.kilimanjaro.intro" to "Africa's highest, and the only continental summit that needs no technical gear, which is why tens of thousands reach it each year. But going from rainforest at 1,800 m to glacier at 5,895 m crosses five vegetation zones, four seasons in four days. Its ice cap is retreating fast, and many researchers expect it gone this century. What you see now may be what the next few generations only read about.",
    "mtn.elbrus.intro" to "Europe's highest, a dormant volcano with twin summits. A cable car takes you to 3,800 m and the rest is snow slope — technically easy, but weather is the opponent: wind, poor visibility, and hypothermia cases every year. It is also the cheapest of the seven summits, which is why it is often treated as the entry point.",
    "mtn.aconcagua.intro" to "South America's highest and the highest in the western hemisphere. The normal Horcones route holds no technical ground at all, only scree and snow slope. That sounds simple until you notice base camp sits at 4,200 m and the summit at 6,961 m. Altitude is the real filter here, and many people stop at the 6,000 m camps.",
    "mtn.denali.intro" to "North America's highest, in Alaska. The official name went back from McKinley to the original Denali in 2015. What sets it apart is not the height but the environment: extreme northern cold, storms that last days, and hauling your own sleds of supplies. At the same altitude it is far colder than a Himalayan peak. Many people treat Denali as where real mountaineering begins.",
    "mtn.vinson.intro" to "Antarctica's highest, buried in the Ellsworth Mountains. It is not on any regular flight path: you fly to an ice runway on the continent, then take a smaller aircraft. It is the most expensive of the seven summits. The route itself is not complex — the difficulty is getting there, and minus forty.",
    "mtn.jaya.intro" to "Oceania's highest, in Indonesian Papua, also called the Carstensz Pyramid. It is one of the few mountains near the equator that still carries a glacier, and that glacier is vanishing at a striking rate. There are only two ways in: five or six days through rainforest, or a helicopter to base camp. Many treat it as the hardest of the seven to arrange — not for the climbing, but for the approach.",
    "mtn.blanc.intro" to "Western Europe's highest, the roof of the Alps, with Chamonix at its foot. It is where modern mountaineering began: Balmat and Paccard in 1786, and nearly every technique in the sport was worked out on its ridges in the two centuries since. The Tour du Mont Blanc circles it, and for many people that loop is the first time they see an alpine glacier up close.",
    "mtn.matterhorn.intro" to "The shape alone is a symbol — a four-sided pyramid, one face to each direction. Whymper's first ascent in 1865 ended with four men falling to their deaths on the descent, and mountaineering has carried that lesson about success and its price ever since. The Hörnli Ridge remains one of the classic routes, and for many it is their first real mountain. The Swiss-Italian border runs across the summit.",

    "mtn.fuji.intro" to "Japan's highest, an active volcano and the country's most direct geographical symbol. It may be the most climbed mountain in the world: four main routes open from July to September, and the Yoshida route needs almost no technical gear. But it is not a tame mountain — summit temperatures can approach freezing, and people are hypothermic up there every year.",
    "mtn.yushan.intro" to "Taiwan's highest, and one of the highest peaks in East Asia. The mountain is slate, and it holds snow in winter. The trailhead is at Tatajia and the route is well maintained, but you need a park permit and a hut booking in advance. Sunrise from the main peak is the classic moment, and for many it is their first dawn at 3,900 m.",
    "mtn.eiger.intro" to "One of the three linked peaks of the Bernese Alps, but its real name comes from the north face. The Eiger Nordwand rises 1,800 m in almost pure rock and mixed ice, first climbed by a four-man team only in 1938; eight people had died on the wall before that. It was called Europe's last problem, and it appears in climbing literature more than any other mountain.",
    "mtn.kinabalu.intro" to "The high point of Borneo, in Sabah, Malaysia. It is granite, not volcanic, and the summit is a bare rock plateau. You leave the hut at two in the morning and climb the final wall on ropes, reaching Low's Peak at dawn. It is probably Asia's most popular night ascent, and for many people the first high-altitude scramble of their lives.",
    "mtn.jungfrau.intro" to "The third of the Bernese trio, linked with Eiger and Mönch in one ridge. What makes it unusual is the railway: the Jungfraubahn runs up inside the rock to 3,454 m, the highest station in Europe. So you can drink coffee above the glacier, or walk up from the valley — one mountain, two completely different experiences.",
    "mtn.teton.intro" to "Wyoming's Grand Teton rises nearly 2,200 m straight out of Jackson Hole. There is no foothill transition, which is why it looks taller than it is. The Exum Ridge is the classic line and is considered one of the most beautiful rock routes in North America.",
    "mtn.rainier.intro" to "A glacier-covered volcano in Washington and the most heavily glaciated peak in the contiguous United States. It is treated as the entry exam for North American glacier climbing: the DC route requires roping up, crossing crevasses and reading avalanche conditions. From Seattle it seems to sit above the clouds almost every day, and it is the first snow mountain for a great many American climbers.",
    "mtn.whitney.intro" to "The highest point in the contiguous United States, in California's Sierra Nevada. The main trail runs 35 km with 1,900 m of gain, done in a day by many and over two by others. Badwater in Death Valley, the lowest point in the country, is less than 130 km away — the highest and lowest points in the US almost touch.",
    "mtn.rosa.intro" to "The second-highest massif in the Alps, on the Swiss-Italian border. It is a group rather than a single peak, and the highest point is the Dufourspitze, named after the Swiss cartographer Dufour. Where the Matterhorn is a spike, Monte Rosa is a wall, with glaciers running from the summit almost to the valley floor. It was among the first Alpine mountains to be systematically surveyed.",
    "mtn.kazbek.intro" to "A dormant volcano in the Caucasus, in Georgia. Halfway up sits the Gergeti Trinity Church at 2,170 m, a national symbol of the country. The route is not hard, but the weather turns fast and there is a glacier to cross. Many people combine it with Elbrus as an introduction to the Caucasus.",
    "mtn.ararat.intro" to "A volcano on the Armenian plateau in eastern Turkey, and a spiritual landmark for the whole region. Tradition holds that Noah's ark came to rest on it after the flood, which is why it appears in almost every version of the story. The climb is snow slope with little technical difficulty, but permits and border controls are the awkward part. From the summit you can see Armenia, Iran and Turkey at once.",
    "mtn.kenya.intro" to "Africa's second-highest, a glacier mountain on the equator. The main peaks Batian and Nelion are technical rock climbs, but the third summit, Point Lenana at 4,985 m, can be reached on foot and is the popular route. The equatorial snowline is retreating, and many people go specifically to record it while it is still there.",
    "mtn.damavand.intro" to "Iran's highest and the highest in the Middle East, an active volcano. The summit holds a small crater that vents sulphur year-round, and that is its signature image. The route is a long volcanic scree slope with no technical ground, but the altitude gain is fast and the wind is strong. It is one of the few five-thousanders in Asia you can simply walk up.",
    "mtn.orizaba.intro" to "Mexico's highest and the third-highest in North America, a dormant volcano. It was a landmark for Spanish navigators — its snow top is visible from the Gulf of Mexico. The route crosses a glacier and is moderately technical, with pronounced altitude effects. In Mexico it is called Citlaltépetl, the mountain of the star.",
    "mtn.chimborazo.intro" to "A dormant volcano in the Ecuadorian Andes. Its summit is not the highest in the world, but it is the farthest point from the centre of the Earth — because of the equatorial bulge, it is more than two kilometres further out than Everest. Many people come for that fact rather than for the climbing.",
    "mtn.illimani.intro" to "The landmark on the skyline of La Paz, Bolivia, four summits in a row. You can see it from the city, which makes it arguably the most visible six-thousander on Earth. The route is snow slope and crevasse travel, a classic South American introduction to high altitude. The Aymara regard it as a guardian mountain.",
    "mtn.sajama.intro" to "Bolivia's highest, a near-perfect cone. It is so symmetrical it looks drawn, and near the summit there are remnants of the highest forest line in South America. The route alternates snow and scree, technically moderate but with obvious altitude. The surrounding altiplano is one of the driest places on the continent.",
    "mtn.huascaran.intro" to "Peru's highest, part of the Cordillera Blanca. In 1970 an earthquake triggered an ice and rock avalanche off its north face that buried the town of Yungay and killed some twenty thousand people — one of the worst natural disasters in climbing history. It remains a South American objective, but glacier retreat changes the route year by year.",
    "mtn.ojos.intro" to "A volcano on the Chile-Argentina border and the highest active volcano on Earth. Its last eruption was around 700 AD, and there are still fumaroles near the summit. The route crosses extremely dry high desert with strong wind, hard UV and almost no water. The most common line in summit logs is that this is the most desolate place I have ever seen.",
    "mtn.elias.intro" to "On the Canada-Alaska border, the second-highest in North America. It rises more than five thousand metres almost directly from sea level, giving it one of the greatest relative reliefs on Earth. The mountain carries one of the largest piedmont glacier systems in the world, and access is by ski plane. Because it is so remote, it sees far fewer ascents than Himalayan peaks of the same height.",
    "mtn.logan.intro" to "Canada's highest, in Yukon. It is one of the largest mountains on Earth by volume — not the tallest, but enormous in bulk. Reaching base camp means more than a hundred kilometres of glacier travel. First climbed in 1925, and for decades afterwards summit records were very few.",
    "mtn.kailash.intro" to "In western Tibet, a mountain held sacred by four religions at once: Tibetan Buddhism, Hinduism, Bon and Jainism. It has never been summited — not because it cannot be, but because it must not; climbing is a religious taboo. The kora is 52 km with the Dolma La at 5,630 m as its high point, and in the Year of the Horse one circuit counts as thirteen.",

    "mtn.helan.intro" to "An isolated north-south range on the Ningxia-Inner Mongolia border, standing between the Yinchuan plain and the desert. The Western Xia dynasty treated it as a sacred mountain, and its tombs and rock art are still in the foothills. Yue Fei's line about riding through the Helan passes made the name better known than the altitude. It is the start of this whole path: 3,556 m.",
    "mtn.taibai.intro" to "The highest peak of the Qinling and the highest point in the eastern half of mainland China. It is also the climate divide between north and south: wet on one slope, dry on the other, two kinds of vegetation on one mountain. At the summit, Daye Hai is a cirque lake left by Quaternary glaciers at 3,590 m. The Aotai traverse is one of China's most famous long routes, and also its most accident-prone.",
    "mtn.jiuding.intro" to "A section of the Minshan in Maoxian, Sichuan, with a main peak close to five thousand metres. The name comes from nine connected summits; the rock is limestone and shale, and stability is poor. Every June the alpine rhododendrons open across the slopes, one of the earliest flower seasons in western Sichuan. Ascents are few — most people stop at the meadows halfway.",
    "mtn.siguniang_da.intro" to "The easiest of the Siguniang group. From Rilong town you can reach the top in a day: gentle slope, no technical ground, and commercial teams use it as a first five-thousander. But it is still five thousand metres, and altitude and weather do not negotiate.",
    "mtn.siguniang_er.intro" to "The second peak is steeper than the first but still walkable. The final push crosses a scree slope where hands are needed. Most people treat it as the step after Da Feng — the same range, a little more exposure. The view of Yaomei from the top is the best on the route.",
    "mtn.siguniang_san.intro" to "From the third peak you need technical gear. The route includes rock walls, snow slopes and an exposed ridge that has to be protected. It is often used as the first lesson in technical mountaineering: not the hardest, but enough to show you what you are missing. Above it stands Yaomei, which is another order of magnitude entirely.",
    "mtn.gangshika.intro" to "In the eastern Qilian near Menyuan, Qinghai. Its glacier is one of the most complete in the range, and one of the few places in China where a glacier terminus is visible in summer. The route is mostly snow slope with significant gain, a common introductory peak in the northwest. In July the fields at its foot are covered in rapeseed flowers.",
    "mtn.nianbao.intro" to "A sacred mountain of the Golok in Qinghai and part of the Sanjiangyuan source region. The landform is a group of glacial rock peaks and lakes, with wildflowers everywhere in summer. The scenic area closed to tourism in 2018, so it can now only be seen from a distance. In local legend it is the brother of Amne Machin.",
    "mtn.baima.intro" to "A section of the Hengduan range in Diqing, Yunnan, whose main peak is Zhalacui. It is the main habitat of the Yunnan snub-nosed monkey and a core area of the Three Parallel Rivers World Heritage site. Climbers know it mostly because the Yunnan-Tibet highway crosses it; the Baima pass is the stretch people remember.",
    "mtn.xuebaoding.intro" to "The main peak of the Minshan in Sichuan and the highest point of that range. It is one of the sacred mountains of Bon, and the summit is a great snow dome. The route has some technical difficulty, mixed glacier and rock, and ascents are few. From the Chengdu plain, on a clear day, it is the easternmost snow peak on the skyline.",
    "mtn.nama.intro" to "A satellite peak of Gongga, the same height as Xuebaoding but a completely different character. It is an independent snow peak southwest of Gongga, and the route is snow slope and crevasses. Because access is good and difficulty moderate, commercial teams use it as the six-thousand-metre step up. From the top you see the whole of Gongga's main summit, which is the main selling point.",
    "mtn.yulong.intro" to "A sacred mountain of the Naxi in Lijiang, Yunnan, thirteen snow peaks in a line. The main summit, Shanzidou, has no recognised ascent — the rock is extremely unstable limestone. From the Lijiang basin it changes colour many times a day, which is why it became a symbol. The village of Yuhu at its foot is the start of several trekking routes.",
    "mtn.altun.intro" to "The Altun range on the Xinjiang-Qinghai border, with its main peak deep in a no-man's land. It is one of China's four great uninhabited regions, and reaching the mountains means crossing hundreds of kilometres of desert. The range is dry with little glacier cover, a classic hyper-arid alpine landscape. Because of ecological protection, crossing and climbing have long been restricted.",
    "mtn.tuanjie.intro" to "The highest peak of the Qilian range, on the Gansu-Qinghai border. The name was given by a survey team in the 1950s; locally it was called Zhaiwujiele. The mountain is typical Qilian: glacier, snow slope and scree in alternation, with no extreme technical ground. Because the approach is long, summit records have always been few.",
    "mtn.yangmaiyong.intro" to "One of the three protectors of Yading, representing Manjushri. Its shape is a near-perfect pyramid, seen most completely from the Luorong pasture. The three sacred peaks — Xiannairi, Yangmaiyong and Chanadorje — surround Milk Lake and Five Colour Lake, and the kora takes three days. It is the most popular trek in western Sichuan.",
    "mtn.xiannairi.intro" to "One of the three protectors of Yading, representing Avalokiteshvara. It is the highest of the three, with a rounded form that contrasts with Yangmaiyong's sharpness. From Chonggu monastery its reflection falls in Pearl Lake, the most photographed image in Yading. The kora starts at its foot.",
    "mtn.tianhaizi.intro" to "An independent snow peak north of Gongga, in Kangding, Sichuan. Its name comes from the alpine lake below it. The route is scree and snow slope, not technically hard, but the altitude gain is fast. It is a landmark on the Gongga circuit, and its south face is visible from the Zimei pass.",
    "mtn.queer.intro" to "A snow mountain beside the northern Sichuan-Tibet highway in Ganzi, called the first obstacle of that road. A Chinese-Japanese team made the first ascent in 1988. Its northwest ridge is a classic introductory technical route: crevasses, snow bridges and steep snow, all present but manageable. Many commercial teams use it as a six-thousand-metre training ground.",
    "mtn.yuzhu.intro" to "The highest peak of the eastern Kunlun and one of the easiest six-thousanders in China. The south route is gentle with almost no crevasses, and is used by many commercial teams. Its significance is firsts: first crampons, first roped travel, first experience of six-thousand-metre oxygen levels.",
    "mtn.genie.intro" to "A sacred mountain of Litang, Sichuan, its main peak ringed by thirteen snow summits. The massif is a huge circular valley, and the kora takes several days. In a Chinese National Geographic survey it was listed as one of the most beautiful mountains in China. Compared with Gongga and Namcha Barwa it sees very few visitors; most of the pilgrims are local.",
    "mtn.yaomei.intro" to "The main peak of Siguniang, called the queen of Sichuan peaks. It is not a mountain you walk up but a granite tower: steep on all four sides, almost no gentle ground, first climbed only in 1981 by a Japanese university team via the south face. In Chinese climbing circles it has long been the dividing line of technical mountaineering.",
    "mtn.amne.intro" to "A sacred mountain of the Golok in Qinghai, its main peak called Maqen Gangri. It is a large massif rather than a single summit, with glaciers on all sides. In Tibetan tradition it ranks alongside Kailash and Kawagarbo, and the kora takes seven or eight days. Climbers remember it for long glacier routes and very unstable weather.",
    "mtn.geladaindong.intro" to "The source of the Tuotuo, the headwater of the Yangtze, in Qinghai. Its meltwater becomes the Tuotuo, then the Tongtian, then the Jinsha — which is why it is called the first mountain of the Yangtze. The massif is large with dense glacier cover, but climbing is strictly limited for ecological reasons.",
    "mtn.kawagarbo.intro" to "The main peak of Meili Snow Mountain in Yunnan, whose Tibetan name means white snow mountain. In 1991 a Chinese-Japanese team of seventeen died on the mountain, one of the worst accidents in climbing history. In 2001 Deqin county made it illegal to climb Kawagarbo, and it remains unclimbed. In local belief it is the first of eight sacred mountains.",
    "mtn.muztagh_ata.intro" to "A high peak of the Kunlun range on the Xinjiang-Qinghai border. The name is easily confused with Muztagh Ata in the Pamirs, but this is a different mountain. The massif is dry with little glacier, ascents are few and the approach is long. It appears more often on Kunlun traverses than on summit lists.",
    "mtn.khan.intro" to "The second-highest peak of the Tianshan, on the China-Kyrgyzstan border. Called the king of the Tianshan, it is a steep snow pyramid. The route is technically hard, the weather is severe, and ascents are far fewer than for peaks of the same height. In Kazakh, Khan Tengri means lord of the sky.",
    "mtn.qungmogangri.intro" to "A section of the Nyainqentanglha range in central Tibet. The massif is broad, with glaciers on both north and south sides, and it is a summer water source for nearby herders. Ascents are few; most teams approach from the south and need long yak transport. In Tibetan the name is associated with the colour blue.",
    "mtn.lenin.intro" to "On the Kyrgyz-Tajik border in the Pamirs. It is the easiest of the seven-thousanders: a long snow slope with no technical ground. First climbed in 1928, it was long the Soviet system's introduction to 7,000 m. But base camp sits at 3,600 m, so summit day involves nearly 3,500 m of gain.",
    "mtn.nyainqen.intro" to "The main peak of the Nyainqentanglha range in Tibet, companion to Lake Namtso. In Tibetan legend the mountain and the lake are a married pair: the peak is the god, the lake the goddess. The summit is glaciated, the route is long, and ascents are extremely few. From the north shore of Namtso its reflection is the classic image of northern Tibet.",
    "mtn.noijin.intro" to "The main peak of the Noijin Kangsang massif in Nagarze county, Tibet. At its foot lies the Karola glacier, one of the few in Tibet you can reach by car. Because access is easy it is where many people see a glacier up close for the first time. But the ice is retreating fast: the viewpoint of ten years ago is now far from the terminus.",
    "mtn.noshaq.intro" to "Afghanistan's highest, in the Hindu Kush. Because of the security situation, summit records in recent decades are very scattered. The route is moderately technical, but access and permits are far more complicated than the climbing. It is the least visited of the seven-thousanders.",
    "mtn.jarabai.intro" to "In Nyingchi, Tibet, facing Namcha Barwa across the Yarlung Tsangpo. Its shape is rounder than Namcha Barwa's, but it is less than five hundred metres lower. From the Sejila pass the two often appear together — one sharp, one round. Ascents are extremely rare; most people only see it from a distance.",
    "mtn.chomolhari.intro" to "A sacred mountain on the Bhutan-Tibet border, the goddess peak of Bhutanese belief. The government has long banned climbing, so mountaineers can only reach a camp at its foot. The shape is a near-symmetrical snow pyramid, seen most completely from Paro Dzong. In Bhutanese legend it is one of five sacred mountains guarding the country.",
    "mtn.tomur.intro" to "The highest peak of the Tianshan in Xinjiang, in Wensu county. It is the main summit of the range, a huge massif with glaciers spilling down all sides. A Chinese team made the first ascent in 1977. The approach is long and crosses a protected area. In Uyghur, Tomur means iron mountain.",
    "mtn.somoniy.intro" to "Tajikistan's highest, in the Pamirs. In Soviet times it was called Communist Peak; it was renamed in 1998 after the country's head of state. It is one of the longer seven-thousanders, requiring multiple carries to high camps. The approach runs east from Dushanbe along the Pamir highway.",
    "mtn.kulha.intro" to "The main peak of the Kula Kangri massif in Shannan, Tibet, with Baimalin Co at its foot. The route is glacier and scree, and the approach takes two days on foot. The lake is an unusual blue-green, fed by glacial melt. It is one of the few mountains in Tibet better known for its trekking than its climbing.",
    "mtn.tirich.intro" to "Pakistan's highest in the Hindu Kush. Its north face drops more than 2,700 m, one of the steepest walls in the world. A Norwegian team made the first ascent in 1950, and the north face route has rarely been repeated. In the Chitral valley below, it is a sacred mountain to local Tajik people.",
    "mtn.gongga.intro" to "Sichuan's highest, called the king of Sichuan peaks. Americans Moore and Burdsall made the first ascent in 1932, and nobody has repeated that route since; the number of successful ascents is far below Everest's. Its difficulty is broken glacier and unstable weather — the clouds of Gongga are what climbers talk about most.",
    "mtn.kongur.intro" to "The main peak of the Kongur massif in the Pamirs of Xinjiang, and one of the highest points of the Pamir plateau. The mountain is a huge ice field, the route long and the glacier complex. A British team made the first ascent in 1981. Because access is hard, its ascent count is very low for a seven-thousander.",
    "mtn.namunani.intro" to "In western Tibet, facing Kailash across Lake Manasarovar. In Buddhist cosmology it is the goddess peak, paired with Kailash. The route is mostly glacier, permits are hard to obtain, and ascents are very few. Seen from the shore of Manasarovar its reflection falls in the sacred lake — the classic view of Ngari.",    "mtn.snow.none" to "No permanent snow",

    "mtn.xueshan.intro" to "Taiwan's second-highest and the main peak of the Xueshan range, one of the few mountains in Taiwan with clear glacial remains. Its cirque is left from the last ice age — rare topography on a subtropical island. The route starts from Wuling Farm and takes two days; it is one of the most popular of Taiwan's hundred peaks.",
    "mtn.muztagh.intro" to "In the Pamirs, called the father of ice mountains. It is the easiest of the seven-thousanders: a long snow slope with no technical ground, first climbed by a joint Chinese-Soviet team in 1956. Many people use it as their first 7,000 m. But easy refers only to the terrain — the high camps are strung out and the wind is strong.",
    "mtn.namcha.intro" to "In Nyingchi, Tibet, at the great bend of the Yarlung Tsangpo. A Chinese-Japanese team made the first ascent in 1992 after decades of failed attempts. It is called the shy maiden peak — for most of the year it hides in cloud, and a day when the whole massif is visible is considered luck. It also has one of the greatest reliefs of any mountain: nearly 5,000 m from valley to summit.",)
